<section class="bhd-page__feature" data-lp-section="dose">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">WHY IT BELONGS</p>
    <h2>Bicarbonate earns its place before dose turns it into a loading protocol.</h2>
    <p><strong>Bicarbonate is the body’s principal extracellular buffer.</strong> During hard exercise, hydrogen ions leave working muscle and are buffered in the extracellular space. Raising blood bicarbonate at higher supplemental doses is one reason sodium bicarbonate has been studied for repeated high-intensity work and time-trial performance.</p>
    <p>It also has a real rehydration lineage. Earlier World Health Organization oral rehydration salts used <strong>sodium bicarbonate as the base component</strong>; modern formulations use citrate largely for better storage stability while preserving the role of base in correcting base-deficit acidosis.</p>
    <p><strong>For a hydration formula, that makes bicarbonate more than label decoration:</strong> it contributes sodium while broadening the anion profile beyond chloride alone, and it adds a physiologically active buffer ion with established exercise and rehydration science behind it.</p>
    <p><strong>Bicarb Bonus™: real buffering chemistry in hydration, without turning the drink into a loading protocol.</strong></p>
    <p><a href="/pages/why-bicarbonate">See the science →</a> · <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34503527/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISSN position stand</a> · <a href="https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/WHO-FCH-CAH-06.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHO oral rehydration salts</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

